1
00:00:00,667 --> 00:00:02,807
In the spring of eighteen twenty,

2
00:00:02,827 --> 00:00:06,687
a Danish professor named Hans Christian Oersted was giving

3
00:00:06,707 --> 00:00:08,097
an evening lecture.

4
00:00:08,267 --> 00:00:11,767
On his bench lay a battery, a length of wire,

5
00:00:11,787 --> 00:00:14,457
and, by chance, a compass.

6
00:00:14,587 --> 00:00:16,257
He closed the circuit.

7
00:00:16,347 --> 00:00:18,497
And the compass needle moved.

8
00:00:18,667 --> 00:00:20,177
Nothing was touching it.

9
00:00:20,267 --> 00:00:21,857
No magnet was near it.

10
00:00:22,267 --> 00:00:26,417
A current in a wire had reached out and turned a magnet.

11
00:00:26,587 --> 00:00:30,167
For two thousand years, people had treated electricity

12
00:00:30,187 --> 00:00:32,817
and magnetism as two separate things.

13
00:00:33,307 --> 00:00:36,087
That evening, in front of a few students,

14
00:00:36,107 --> 00:00:38,337
the wall between them cracked.

15
00:00:38,507 --> 00:00:42,177
To see why it mattered, we have to go back to the beginning.

16
00:00:43,533 --> 00:00:46,633
The ancient Greeks knew of a dark stone,

17
00:00:46,653 --> 00:00:48,953
found near a place called Magnesia,

18
00:00:48,973 --> 00:00:50,953
that pulled on iron.

19
00:00:50,973 --> 00:00:54,963
We call it lodestone, and from Magnesia we get the word

20
00:00:55,133 --> 00:00:55,843
magnet.

21
00:00:56,253 --> 00:01:00,313
In China, a sliver of lodestone was found to turn until

22
00:01:00,333 --> 00:01:02,363
it lay north and south.

23
00:01:02,493 --> 00:01:06,193
By the year ten eighty-eight, the scholar Shen Kuo had

24
00:01:06,213 --> 00:01:10,153
described a magnetised needle used to find direction,

25
00:01:10,173 --> 00:01:13,443
and noted that it did not point exactly true.

26
00:01:13,613 --> 00:01:17,603
Within a century, sailors were crossing open sea by it.

27
00:01:17,773 --> 00:01:21,433
The first careful experiments came in twelve sixty-nine,

28
00:01:21,453 --> 00:01:23,593
from a French soldier and engineer,

29
00:01:23,613 --> 00:01:25,153
Peter Peregrinus.

30
00:01:25,173 --> 00:01:28,953
He laid a needle on a round lodestone at many places,

31
00:01:28,973 --> 00:01:32,073
traced the lines it followed, and found that they all

32
00:01:32,093 --> 00:01:33,753
met at two points.

33
00:01:33,773 --> 00:01:35,873
He named them poles.

34
00:01:35,893 --> 00:01:39,633
He showed that like poles push apart and unlike poles

35
00:01:39,653 --> 00:01:40,793
pull together.

36
00:01:40,813 --> 00:01:43,073
And he found something strange:

37
00:01:43,093 --> 00:01:46,473
break a magnet in half, and you do not get one pole on

38
00:01:46,493 --> 00:01:47,593
each piece.

39
00:01:47,613 --> 00:01:50,003
You get two complete magnets.

40
00:01:50,173 --> 00:01:53,353
Then, in sixteen hundred, William Gilbert,

41
00:01:53,373 --> 00:01:56,113
the same man who gave us the word electric,

42
00:01:56,133 --> 00:01:59,713
carved a lodestone into a small sphere and studied how

43
00:01:59,733 --> 00:02:02,003
a needle behaved on its surface.

44
00:02:02,093 --> 00:02:05,603
It behaved exactly as a compass does on the Earth.

45
00:02:05,693 --> 00:02:07,523
His conclusion was bold.

46
00:02:07,853 --> 00:02:10,803
The Earth itself is a great magnet.

47
00:02:12,133 --> 00:02:16,163
Every magnet has two poles, north and south.

48
00:02:16,293 --> 00:02:19,963
And here magnetism differs sharply from electricity.

49
00:02:20,293 --> 00:02:22,923
A positive charge can exist alone.

50
00:02:23,013 --> 00:02:24,363
So can a negative one.

51
00:02:24,693 --> 00:02:28,363
But nobody has ever found a north pole on its own.

52
00:02:28,533 --> 00:02:30,953
Cut a magnet as small as you like,

53
00:02:30,973 --> 00:02:33,243
and each piece still has both.

54
00:02:33,653 --> 00:02:34,763
That is a clue.

55
00:02:35,093 --> 00:02:38,513
It tells us that magnetism does not come from some kind

56
00:02:38,533 --> 00:02:39,963
of magnetic charge.

57
00:02:40,213 --> 00:02:41,963
It comes from something else.

58
00:02:42,373 --> 00:02:43,883
Hold on to that question.

59
00:02:45,233 --> 00:02:49,023
Like a charge, a magnet changes the space around it.

60
00:02:49,153 --> 00:02:53,293
Scatter iron filings near one and they line up along curves,

61
00:02:53,313 --> 00:02:56,103
showing a pattern that was there all along.

62
00:02:56,273 --> 00:02:58,653
That pattern is the magnetic field,

63
00:02:58,673 --> 00:03:00,663
and we write it with the letter B.

64
00:03:01,153 --> 00:03:03,943
We draw it with lines, as Faraday taught us.

65
00:03:04,353 --> 00:03:07,533
By convention, they leave the north pole and enter the

66
00:03:07,553 --> 00:03:08,103
south.

67
00:03:08,433 --> 00:03:11,693
A compass needle placed anywhere in the field turns to

68
00:03:11,713 --> 00:03:14,343
lie along the line through that point.

69
00:03:14,513 --> 00:03:17,703
But there is one great difference from the electric field.

70
00:03:18,113 --> 00:03:21,533
Electric field lines start on a positive charge and end

71
00:03:21,553 --> 00:03:22,983
on a negative one.

72
00:03:23,153 --> 00:03:26,823
Magnetic field lines never start and never end.

73
00:03:26,913 --> 00:03:29,933
They pass through the magnet and close on themselves,

74
00:03:29,953 --> 00:03:31,743
in unbroken loops.

75
00:03:31,873 --> 00:03:33,853
That is the same fact as before,

76
00:03:33,873 --> 00:03:37,573
seen another way: there are no single poles for the lines

77
00:03:37,593 --> 00:03:39,303
to begin or end on.

78
00:03:40,767 --> 00:03:42,997
Now we can see what Oersted found.

79
00:03:43,407 --> 00:03:46,747
A wire carrying a current is surrounded by a magnetic

80
00:03:46,767 --> 00:03:47,597
field.

81
00:03:47,727 --> 00:03:50,187
The field does not point toward the wire,

82
00:03:50,207 --> 00:03:51,317
or away from it.

83
00:03:51,647 --> 00:03:54,517
It circles the wire, in rings.

84
00:03:54,687 --> 00:03:57,077
There is an easy way to remember the direction.

85
00:03:57,487 --> 00:03:59,467
Grip the wire in your right hand,

86
00:03:59,487 --> 00:04:01,957
with your thumb pointing along the current.

87
00:04:02,047 --> 00:04:04,757
Your fingers curl the way the field goes.

88
00:04:05,407 --> 00:04:07,637
The news reached Paris in September.

89
00:04:07,967 --> 00:04:10,587
André-Marie Ampère saw it demonstrated,

90
00:04:10,607 --> 00:04:13,357
and within one week he had gone further.

91
00:04:13,487 --> 00:04:15,627
If a current acts like a magnet,

92
00:04:15,647 --> 00:04:19,317
he reasoned, then two currents should act on each other.

93
00:04:19,487 --> 00:04:21,797
He set up two parallel wires.

94
00:04:21,887 --> 00:04:23,907
With the currents flowing the same way,

95
00:04:23,927 --> 00:04:25,677
the wires pulled together.

96
00:04:25,807 --> 00:04:28,837
With the currents opposed, they pushed apart.

97
00:04:29,007 --> 00:04:31,397
No iron, no lodestone.

98
00:04:31,447 --> 00:04:33,907
Only electricity in motion.

99
00:04:33,927 --> 00:04:37,267
That same autumn, Biot and Savart measured the strength

100
00:04:37,287 --> 00:04:38,507
of the field.

101
00:04:38,527 --> 00:04:41,707
Around a long straight wire, it grows with the current

102
00:04:41,727 --> 00:04:43,867
and falls off with distance.

103
00:04:43,887 --> 00:04:46,827
One ampere, at a distance of one centimetre,

104
00:04:46,847 --> 00:04:49,627
gives about twenty millionths of a tesla:

105
00:04:49,647 --> 00:04:52,907
roughly half the strength of the Earth's own field.

106
00:04:52,927 --> 00:04:56,427
Ampère drew the conclusion that changed everything.

107
00:04:56,447 --> 00:04:59,347
Magnetism is not a separate force.

108
00:04:59,367 --> 00:05:01,877
It is what moving charge does.

109
00:05:03,233 --> 00:05:06,733
Bend the wire into a loop, and the rings of field add

110
00:05:06,753 --> 00:05:07,783
up through the middle.

111
00:05:08,273 --> 00:05:11,053
One side of the loop behaves like a north pole,

112
00:05:11,073 --> 00:05:12,663
the other like a south.

113
00:05:12,993 --> 00:05:15,543
A loop of current is a magnet.

114
00:05:15,713 --> 00:05:18,773
Wind many loops side by side into a coil,

115
00:05:18,793 --> 00:05:22,103
called a solenoid, and their fields add together.

116
00:05:22,273 --> 00:05:25,863
Inside, the field runs straight and nearly uniform.

117
00:05:26,193 --> 00:05:29,823
Outside, it is exactly the field of a bar magnet.

118
00:05:29,953 --> 00:05:33,013
The more turns in each metre, and the more current,

119
00:05:33,033 --> 00:05:34,623
the stronger it is.

120
00:05:34,753 --> 00:05:37,183
Then put a bar of iron inside.

121
00:05:37,313 --> 00:05:40,973
In eighteen twenty-five, William Sturgeon did just that,

122
00:05:40,993 --> 00:05:45,093
and his small coil lifted twenty times its own weight.

123
00:05:45,113 --> 00:05:47,986
Six years later, the American Joseph Henry,

124
00:05:48,006 --> 00:05:50,533
winding many layers of insulated wire,

125
00:05:50,553 --> 00:05:53,543
built an electromagnet that held up more than a ton.

126
00:05:53,953 --> 00:05:56,743
A magnet you can switch on and off.

127
00:05:56,833 --> 00:06:01,693
Every relay, every contactor, every motor and every transformer

128
00:06:01,713 --> 00:06:02,903
begins here.

129
00:06:04,267 --> 00:06:08,457
So why does iron do this, when copper and wood do not?

130
00:06:08,587 --> 00:06:11,657
And where is the current in a lump of lodestone?

131
00:06:11,787 --> 00:06:15,247
Ampère guessed the answer, long before anyone could check

132
00:06:15,267 --> 00:06:15,817
it.

133
00:06:15,947 --> 00:06:19,847
He proposed that inside a magnet there are countless tiny

134
00:06:19,867 --> 00:06:21,177
loops of current.

135
00:06:21,307 --> 00:06:22,417
He was right.

136
00:06:22,587 --> 00:06:26,287
Electrons circle their atoms, and they also spin,

137
00:06:26,307 --> 00:06:29,527
and each one acts as a minute magnet.

138
00:06:29,547 --> 00:06:32,047
In most materials these cancel out.

139
00:06:32,067 --> 00:06:34,647
But in iron, nickel and cobalt,

140
00:06:34,667 --> 00:06:37,367
neighbouring atoms line up with each other.

141
00:06:37,387 --> 00:06:40,567
In nineteen hundred and seven, Pierre Weiss showed that

142
00:06:40,587 --> 00:06:43,847
they do so in patches, called domains.

143
00:06:43,867 --> 00:06:46,967
In an ordinary piece of iron, the domains point in all

144
00:06:46,987 --> 00:06:50,327
directions, and the whole is not a magnet.

145
00:06:50,347 --> 00:06:54,047
Apply a field, and the domains swing into line.

146
00:06:54,067 --> 00:06:57,047
The iron's own magnetism adds to the field,

147
00:06:57,067 --> 00:06:59,297
hundreds or thousands of times over.

148
00:06:59,787 --> 00:07:03,937
That is why an iron core makes an electromagnet so strong.

149
00:07:04,027 --> 00:07:06,097
And it explains our earlier puzzle.

150
00:07:06,427 --> 00:07:09,367
Cut a magnet in half, and you are only cutting between

151
00:07:09,387 --> 00:07:10,497
loops of current.

152
00:07:10,907 --> 00:07:13,577
Each piece still has both faces.

153
00:07:13,707 --> 00:07:15,737
Heat destroys the order.

154
00:07:15,867 --> 00:07:19,207
In eighteen ninety-five, Pierre Curie found that above

155
00:07:19,227 --> 00:07:22,247
a certain temperature, seven hundred and seventy degrees

156
00:07:22,267 --> 00:07:25,047
for iron, the alignment collapses,

157
00:07:25,067 --> 00:07:27,537
and a magnet stops being a magnet.

158
00:07:28,900 --> 00:07:32,400
A magnetic field does not push on a charge that is standing

159
00:07:32,420 --> 00:07:33,250
still.

160
00:07:33,380 --> 00:07:36,410
It acts only on a charge that is moving.

161
00:07:36,580 --> 00:07:40,240
And the force is strange: it points neither along the

162
00:07:40,260 --> 00:07:44,970
field nor along the motion, but at right angles to both.

163
00:07:45,140 --> 00:07:48,560
A force that is always sideways cannot speed a charge

164
00:07:48,580 --> 00:07:50,250
up or slow it down.

165
00:07:50,500 --> 00:07:52,450
It can only turn it.

166
00:07:52,660 --> 00:07:56,600
So a charge moving across a magnetic field travels in

167
00:07:56,620 --> 00:07:57,450
a circle.

168
00:07:57,940 --> 00:08:01,250
A current is nothing but charges in motion.

169
00:08:01,380 --> 00:08:06,130
So a wire carrying current across a field is pushed sideways.

170
00:08:06,260 --> 00:08:09,440
The force is the field, times the current,

171
00:08:09,460 --> 00:08:11,810
times the length of wire in the field.

172
00:08:11,940 --> 00:08:15,040
Put a loop of wire in a field and pass a current through

173
00:08:15,060 --> 00:08:15,450
it.

174
00:08:15,860 --> 00:08:18,640
One side is pushed up, the other down,

175
00:08:18,660 --> 00:08:20,210
and the loop turns.

176
00:08:20,340 --> 00:08:22,810
You are looking at an electric motor.

177
00:08:24,167 --> 00:08:27,197
To build with magnetism, we have to measure it.

178
00:08:27,607 --> 00:08:30,947
In eighteen thirty-two, the mathematician Carl Friedrich

179
00:08:30,967 --> 00:08:34,787
Gauss worked out how to measure the Earth's field in absolute

180
00:08:34,807 --> 00:08:38,637
terms, using only length, mass and time.

181
00:08:38,807 --> 00:08:41,667
The old unit of field carries his name.

182
00:08:41,687 --> 00:08:45,277
The modern unit is the tesla, after Nikola Tesla,

183
00:08:45,327 --> 00:08:48,867
adopted by international agreement in nineteen sixty.

184
00:08:48,887 --> 00:08:51,507
One tesla is ten thousand gauss,

185
00:08:51,527 --> 00:08:53,827
and it is a strong field.

186
00:08:53,847 --> 00:08:57,387
The Earth's field is about fifty millionths of a tesla.

187
00:08:57,407 --> 00:08:59,987
A fridge magnet, a few thousandths.

188
00:09:00,007 --> 00:09:03,667
A hospital scanner, between one and a half and three.

189
00:09:03,687 --> 00:09:07,067
And magnetism gave us the unit of current itself.

190
00:09:07,087 --> 00:09:10,787
From nineteen forty-eight, the ampere was defined by Ampère's

191
00:09:10,807 --> 00:09:13,267
own experiment: the current which,

192
00:09:13,287 --> 00:09:16,427
in two long parallel wires a metre apart,

193
00:09:16,447 --> 00:09:19,027
produces a set force between them.

194
00:09:19,047 --> 00:09:21,667
It stayed that way until twenty nineteen,

195
00:09:21,687 --> 00:09:25,357
when the ampere was redefined by counting electrons.

196
00:09:26,700 --> 00:09:28,530
So let's put it together.

197
00:09:28,700 --> 00:09:33,170
Magnets have two poles, and the poles always come in pairs.

198
00:09:33,260 --> 00:09:36,960
A magnet fills the space around it with a magnetic field,

199
00:09:36,980 --> 00:09:39,330
whose lines form closed loops.

200
00:09:39,420 --> 00:09:43,090
A current makes a magnetic field that circles the wire.

201
00:09:43,180 --> 00:09:46,210
Magnetism is what moving charge does.

202
00:09:46,300 --> 00:09:48,600
A coil concentrates that field,

203
00:09:48,620 --> 00:09:50,930
and an iron core multiplies it,

204
00:09:50,980 --> 00:09:54,680
because iron is full of tiny current loops that line up.

205
00:09:54,700 --> 00:09:58,000
A magnetic field pushes sideways on moving charge,

206
00:09:58,020 --> 00:10:00,720
and that push turns every motor.

207
00:10:00,740 --> 00:10:03,760
So a current makes a magnetic field.

208
00:10:03,780 --> 00:10:06,640
Next time, we ask what happens when that field has to

209
00:10:06,660 --> 00:10:09,000
be built up, and torn down again.

210
00:10:09,020 --> 00:10:11,560
The answer is called inductance.

211
00:10:11,580 --> 00:10:14,840
If this helped you see it, and not just calculate it,

212
00:10:14,860 --> 00:10:17,890
please like, share, and subscribe to this channel.
